From 2ac97422a636ac015fed7e548c96a98da459a939 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael <asoftwaredeveloper@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 10:32:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add client accounts, AI cost estimates, contracts, and
 Stripe deposits
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Clients now sign up (Supabase Auth) before using the AI Discovery
Center, and every session/summary/chat/upload route is scoped to the
signed-in owner so one client can never read another's conversation
(the chat API previously had no access control at all). Building on
that: the AI drafts a cost-estimate range for a completed summary,
a Senior Software Architect reviews and approves pricing before a
contract is ever shown to the client, the client signs it in-app,
and pays a 50% deposit via Stripe Checkout before work starts.

- prisma: Contract/Payment models, ProjectSummary estimate columns,
  DiscoverySession.userId, hand-written migration SQL
- src/lib/supabase: browser/server/middleware clients (@supabase/ssr)
- src/proxy.ts: auth gate for /discovery, /contract, /account and
  their APIs; /admin keeps its existing Basic auth path untouched
- src/lib/ai/estimate.ts: cost-estimate draft on a cheaper model,
  kept separate from the client-facing prompts that must never quote
  a price directly
- src/app/api/contracts, .../sign, .../checkout,
  src/app/api/webhooks/stripe: draft -> human review -> sign -> pay,
  webhook is the only place a payment is ever marked paid
- src/app/{login,signup,account,contract}, src/components/{auth,contract}:
  client-facing auth and contract/payment UI
- src/app/admin/contracts, src/components/admin: staff review queue
- Dockerfile: npm install instead of npm ci (package-lock.json needs
  a local `npm install` + commit to fully re-pin; see Dockerfile
  comment), new NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_* build args
- coolify-compose.yaml, .env.example, next.config.ts: new env vars
  wired through to build args, runtime environment, and CSP connect-src

Contract wording is a starting template, not legal advice — needs a
lawyer's review before real client use. Click-to-sign is a lightweight
audit trail (typed name + hash + IP/UA + timestamp), not a certified
e-signature platform.

